# 311 — A file face: trees as objects, and `storefs`, a VFS view of a named root — done

**Type:** main task · **Owner:** `STO` track · **Priority:** P2 · **Status:** done (2026-11-12) · **Blocked by:** [307](../issues/307-the-stores-own-partition.md) · **Roadmap:** track B "file view over the store" · **Constitution:** MC-4.1, MC-4.7, MC-4.9, MC-11.10

From the kernel session's draft (`STO/2026-10-25T1557Z-KRN-draft-plan-the-store-as-a-minds-memory.md` on `internal-dev-messages`), taken by the storage session at the maintainer's word (2026-10-27). Everything here is plan, not a guarantee (MC-12.3).

## Problem

Programs keep their data as files on FAT, which has no history, no snapshots, no checksums and no rights finer than a zone. The store has all of these, but no file view.

## Plan

- **A directory node:** a canonical DAG-CBOR node of sorted entries (name, kind, CID, size); large directories sharded.
- **`storefs`,** a `vfs.wit` provider that mounts a named root:
  - reads go straight to the store; writes go into a working tree in memory (MC-4.9);
  - **`fsync` and a close publish** a new root by compare-and-swap, as programs written for files expect; an explicit **`commit`** publishes several files' changes at once (the storage session's choice, 2026-10-27);
  - a conflict is reported to the writer, never merged silently.
- **History:** old roots are snapshots; a directory's history and two roots can be compared.
- **Rights:** a mount is a capability for a subtree (MC-4.7).
- **What moves:** the programs' own data directories (000-KRN-0170) can live here. FAT stays for the boot volume and for exchange with other systems, as a projection with an authorized import (MC-11.10).
- `APP`'s part, a request: `fm` and the shell over `storefs`.

## Tasks

- [311-STO-0027](311-STO-0027-directories-as-objects.done): directories as objects, their walk, and the store's reachability through them. Done 2026-10-30.
- [311-STO-0029](311-STO-0029-a-working-tree-published-by-compare-and-swap.done): a working tree in memory, published by compare-and-swap; `blocks tree`. Done 2026-10-30.
- [311-STO-0032](311-STO-0032-storefs-a-vfs-view-of-a-named-root.done): `storefs`, a `vfs.wit` view of a named root, at boot since 311-KRN-0190. Done 2026-11-04.
- [311-STO-0037](311-STO-0037-a-scope-of-a-store-volume.done): a scope of a store volume, a subtree as a capability, read-only or writable, so the shell can lend a program a client. Done 2026-11-04.
- [311-STO-0039](311-STO-0039-two-roots-compared.done): two roots of a tree compared, and a name's kept versions read. Done 2026-11-04.

## Progress (2026-11-04)

- **The acceptance criteria are met** on x86 and aarch64 in QEMU, in `main` e28cfe4d: the shell's file commands on a store volume (the `shell` suite's `storefs_check`), two writers with one told of the conflict, and a writer cut while its data is held leaving the old root (the `store` suite's `storefs_writers_check`). A cut inside a flush's writes to the medium runs in the host tests only.
- **Two roots compared, and a name's kept versions read** (311-STO-0039): in `main` 4130cff6.
- **Still open in the plan:**
  - the programs' data directories in the store (000-KRN-0170, the kernel track's);
  - `fm` over `storefs` (APP's).

## Acceptance criteria

- QEMU: the shell's file commands on a `storefs` mount; a cut in the middle of a publish leaves the old root; two writers, one of them told of the conflict.

## Resolution

**The main task's criteria are met,** on x86 and aarch64 in QEMU, and its five tasks are done:
- the shell's file commands on a `storefs` mount (the `shell` suite's `storefs_check`);
- a writer cut while its data is held leaves the old root;
- of two writers, one is told of the conflict (the `store` suite's `storefs_writers_check`).

A cut inside a flush's writes to the medium runs in the host tests only (Progress, 2026-11-04).

**What the plan names that is not this track's, split off on 2026-11-12:**
- **`fm` over a store volume** (APP's): asked in [requests-APP.md](../issues/requests-APP.md). The shell's part is done (311-APP-0121).
- **A program's own data directory in the store** (the loader's, KRN's): asked on 2026-11-12, [311-KRN-0237](../issues/311-KRN-0237-a-programs-own-directory-in-the-store.md). 000-KRN-0170 gives it on the boot volume's FAT today. STO's check follows once the loader can give a `storefs` scope.

**Not done here:** sharded directories, which a directory past one block would need (docs/storage, "Directories as objects"). No program writes one yet, so no task is open for it.

These are tests of the configurations named, not a proof (MC-12.2). No real machine has run them.

